Difference between ENTRYPOINT and CMD

Most people think they understand ENTRYPOINT and CMD.
They don’t. They memorize definitions and still mess up Dockerfiles.

Let’s fix that.

CMD

CMD defines default arguments for a container.

👉 It’s easy to override at runtime.

Example:

CMD ["echo", "Hello"]

Run:

docker run my-image echo Hi

Result?
Docker replaces CMD. Your default is gone.

Use CMD when:

You want a suggested default

You expect users to override behavior

The container is flexible by design



✅ENTRYPOINT

ENTRYPOINT defines the actual executable.

👉 It’s not meant to be replaced, only extended.

Example:

ENTRYPOINT ["echo"]

Run:

docker run my-image Hello

Result?
Docker appends arguments. echo Hello

Use ENTRYPOINT when:

The container has a single, fixed purpose

You want to enforce behavior

You’re building CLI-style containers


✅ The Power Combo (What Pros Use)

ENTRYPOINT ["python", "app.py"]
CMD ["--help"]

ENTRYPOINT → what always runs

CMD → default arguments (override-friendly)


This is not optional knowledge.
If you’re writing Dockerfiles without understanding this difference, you’re guessing — not engineering.

Docker rewards precision.
Stop memorizing. Start understanding.
